---
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
---

<MarketingLayout
	title="AI for Impact | People/Purpose/Product"
	description="Our stance on AI: a lever for mission-driven work, not a replacement for domain expertise or human judgment."
>
	<section class="page-header">
		<h1>AI for impact,<br />not extraction.</h1>
		<p class="subhead">
			Here's what we actually believe about AI, and how it shapes the way we work.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="content-section">
		<h2>Our position</h2>
		<p>
			AI is the most significant shift in who can build software since cloud computing.
			That's not hype. It's a structural change. Organizations that couldn't afford a
			development team two years ago can now build real products. The question is whether
			they'll build ones that serve people, or ones that extract from them.
		</p>
		<p>
			We think domain expertise is the differentiator. AI amplifies whatever is already there.
			If you know your community, your programs, and your problem deeply, AI makes you faster,
			more capable, and harder to replicate. If you don't, AI just makes you faster at building
			the wrong thing.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="content-section">
		<h2>What AI changes</h2>
		<p>
			For mission-driven organizations, the practical change is this: the cost of building
			a custom digital product has dropped significantly. Things that used to require a
			full engineering team and a six-figure budget are now within reach for organizations
			with modest technical capacity.
		</p>
		<p>
			That opens up options. You don't have to choose between an expensive custom build and
			a vendor product that wasn't designed for your population. You can build something
			that fits, and own it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="content-section">
		<h2>What AI doesn't change</h2>
		<p>
			Judgment. Relationships. Community trust. The hard-won knowledge of what your
			constituents actually need versus what they ask for.
		</p>
		<p>
			AI doesn't know your programs, your population, or the decade of context your team
			carries. It doesn't replace the product thinking that turns a feature list into
			something people actually use. And it has no stake in the outcome. You do.
		</p>
		<p>
			Tools that promise to automate away that expertise are selling something. We're not.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="content-section">
		<h2>How we use it</h2>
		<p>
			AI is woven into how we work, not bolted on as a feature. We use it to move faster,
			explore more options, and reduce the cost of iteration. But every significant decision
			still comes back to humans who understand the problem.
		</p>
		<p>
			More importantly, we teach clients to use it. By the time an engagement ends,
			your team should be able to maintain, extend, and adapt what we built together,
			without coming back to us. Dependency is the failure mode we're actively trying to avoid.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="content-section closing">
		<p>If this framing resonates, let's talk.</p>
		<a href="/work-with-us" class="cta-button">Work with us →</a>
	</section>
</MarketingLayout>

<style>
	.page-header {
		padding: 3em 0 2.5em;
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
		margin-bottom: 3em;
	}
	.page-header h1 {
		font-size: 3rem;
		line-height: 1.1;
		margin-bottom: 0.4em;
	}
	.subhead {
		font-size: 1.15rem;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		max-width: 600px;
	}

	.content-section {
		margin-bottom: 3em;
		padding-bottom: 3em;
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
		max-width: 720px;
	}
	.content-section h2 {
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		margin-bottom: 0.75em;
	}

	.closing {
		text-align: left;
		border-bottom: none;
	}
	.closing p {
		font-size: 1.2rem;
		margin-bottom: 1em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray-dark));
	}

	.cta-button {
		display: inline-block;
		background: var(--accent);
		color: white;
		padding: 0.75em 1.5em;
		border-radius: 4px;
		text-decoration: none;
		font-weight: 700;
		font-size: 1rem;
		transition: background 0.2s ease;
	}
	.cta-button:hover {
		background: var(--accent-dark);
		color: white;
	}

	@media (max-width: 720px) {
		.page-header h1 {
			font-size: 2.25rem;
		}
	}
</style>
